{
 "_comment": "Every steward mark found for a through-route in trail_emblems.json, where it was found, and how much of the badge it actually fills (#1543). NO IMAGE BYTES ARE COMMITTED WITH THIS FILE and that is the point of it: pipeline/tests/test_org_marks.py requires every trail mark in client/src/design-system/assets/trails/ to carry a recorded basis, and 33 of the 35 rows below have none - nobody has asked these organisations and none of them has answered. This file is the research, held where the next session can act on it the day a grant arrives, rather than 33 trademarks entering a public AGPL tree on nobody's authority.",
 "_how_to_use_a_row": "When an organisation grants permission: fetch `found_at`, drop the file into client/src/design-system/assets/trails/, add the org's `trail_mark_in_tree` record in sources.json's org_marks block with its basis, scope and date, and register it in client/src/lib/trails.ts. map/trailBadges.ts needs no change - BADGE_MARK_BY_SOURCE becomes a registry lookup and the mark appears.",
 "_ink_at_18px": "MEASURED 2026-09-29. Each mark was letterboxed into an 18-pixel square on #fffdf7 - TRAIL_BADGE_PLATE_DAY's paper - and every one of the 324 pixels scored for WCAG contrast against it. `ink_at_18px` is the share reaching 3:1 or better; `peak_contrast` is the strongest single pixel. The bar is not picked: the A.T. marker already in the tree measures 0.18 ink at 7.4:1, and `clears_the_at` says whether a mark puts at least that much on the plate. A mark below it is not unusable, it is thinner than the one nobody has complained about.",
 "_why_the_thin_ones_are_thin": "Almost always because the mark is mostly white, and white on the plate's own paper is nothing. That is a fact about the plate as much as the mark, and it is unresolved: whether a pale mark gets a darker plate is a decision nobody has made. @unvalidated - what would settle it is looking at one on a phone in sunlight, which is #105.",
 "_not_found": {
  "_comment": "The 31 through-routes with no mark here, and what is actually in the way. Grouped because the reasons are not the same and only some are fixable. `_resolved` at the foot is the other direction - a group that emptied and why, kept so the next sweep does not re-derive a blocker that has already been answered.",
  "federally_protected": {
   "trails": [
    "pohe",
    "natr"
   ],
   "detail": "The last two National Scenic Trails. Markers prescribed in the Federal Register and protected by 18 U.S.C. 701; NPS trail pages serve the arrowhead instead. An ask to each trail's administrator."
  },
  "no_per_trail_emblem": {
   "trails": [
    "jmt",
    "hst",
    "backbone",
    "big-seki"
   ],
   "detail": "NPS administers all four and publishes none. johnmuirtrail.org is a fan store whose favicon is a stock photo of a shoe."
  },
  "blocked_not_absent": {
   "trails": [
    "colorado-trail",
    "mst"
   ],
   "detail": "coloradotrail.org returns 403 to the page, the JSON API, the favicon and the apple-touch-icon alike. mountainstoseatrail.org challenges every logo request; its favicon slips through and is the stock WordPress logo."
  },
  "site_compromised": {
   "trails": [
    "pinhoti"
   ],
   "detail": "The Pinhoti Trail Alliance domain serves gambling spam - the header image's alt text reads GACOR188. Worth telling somebody. The trail's own mark is a turkey track."
  },
  "domain_dead_or_photographic": {
   "trails": [
    "gwt",
    "black-canyon",
    "bst",
    "quehanna",
    "donut-hole",
    "laurel",
    "mid-state-pa",
    "msg"
   ],
   "detail": "Dead domains, a header baked into a flat photograph, a wordmark tile, or - for the Monadnock-Sunapee Greenway - a landscape illustration measuring 0.02 ink, the thinnest thing tested."
  },
  "club_mark_only": {
   "trails": [
    "knobstone",
    "odt",
    "sd-centennial",
    "baker"
   ],
   "detail": "The steward publishes a club wordmark and no trail symbol. A club's mark is not the trail's, so these keep an empty slot rather than wear somebody else's badge."
  },
  "no_steward_no_mark": {
   "trails": [
    "hayduke",
    "sierra-high",
    "lowest-highest",
    "hot-springs",
    "ect",
    "solomons",
    "sky-islands",
    "nnm-loop",
    "se-serpentine",
    "ahr"
   ],
   "detail": "Routes rather than trails: no organisation, and no mark anybody drew. Two of this group - the Grand Enchantment and the Mogollon Rim - turned out to have one after all, which is why the rest were worth checking twice."
  },
  "_resolved": {
   "pct": "2026-09-30. `permission_stated` held one trail and is gone. PCTA's media page still requires written permission for the PCT emblem, the PCT diamond and the PCTA logo, and still nobody has written - what changed is that the mark did not have to come from PCTA. The US Forest Service administers the trail and publishes the emblem on its own page, so the row below is the federal copy under 17 U.S.C. 105. sources.json's `org_marks.trail_marks.pct` carries the basis, including the insignia question that copyright does not answer."
  }
 },
